window.docContents[240].resolve({"/Foundations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Foundational-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-foundations":{"contents":"Formalized: completed finite-exponent integer-order graph spaces,\nmollification and density, first-order extension on operational \ndomains, finite-exponent trace operators, Hilbert quotient trace spaces and\ntheir minimal-energy extensions on flat and operational  domains,\ncanonical agreement of the finite-exponent and Hilbert trace theories at\n, zero-boundary spaces, Poincaré, Hardy, Sobolev and Morrey inequalities, the\nfull strict subcritical Rellich–Kondrachov range, weak product and pullback\ncalculus, and the integer-order comparison with Fourier-defined Sobolev\nspaces. On bounded operational  domains, arbitrary-offset\none-Lipschitz  scalar maps with derivative bounded by one define\ncompleted finite-exponent Nemytskii operations and satisfy the weak chain\nrule. Arbitrary ordered interval truncations are completed operations on\nthese bounded domains and satisfy the sharp open-interval derivative formula.\nFor every , every zero-preserving real Lipschitz map acts\non  and satisfies the full almost-everywhere weak\nchain rule with its classical derivative. On bounded operational \ndomains, every real Lipschitz scalar map acts on  without\na zero-preserving hypothesis and satisfies the same chain rule.\n\nHigher-order bounded extension is also formalized for every positive integer\n on bounded open domains with  graph boundary, for every\n. The construction from only an operational \natlas remains first order. The global one-dimensional  theory has\nan absolutely continuous representative, almost-everywhere classical\nderivative, and finite-kink calculus for absolute value, positive and negative\nparts, and interval truncations. In every positive dimension, each global\n class has an explicit absolutely continuous representative on\nalmost every line parallel to every coordinate axis, with the corresponding\nweak derivative as its classical derivative almost everywhere.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Foundational Coverage and Open Problems","header":"3.5.1.4. Sobolev foundations","id":"/Foundations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Foundational-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-foundations"},"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--Solution-concepts":{"contents":"Suppose the initial temperature on the rod has a jump. Finite sine sums still\ngive smooth approximate evolutions, but their limit at time zero need not be\ndifferentiable. Requiring the same pointwise calculus at every stage would\nexclude the datum we wanted to study. We need a meaning of differentiation\nthat makes sense before smoothing has occurred.\n\nOur sine mode is a classical solution: all derivatives in the equation exist\nin the ordinary sense. Classical regularity is often the conclusion we hope\nto recover from an existence proof. It can be too much to demand of an\napproximation limit, and some nonlinear equations destroy it during the\nevolution.\n\nA distributional solution tests the equation against smooth compactly\nsupported functions and moves derivatives by integration by parts. If  and\n are locally integrable,  in distributions means\n\nThe unknown no longer needs two pointwise derivatives. The test function\nsupplies them, and its compact support removes the boundary terms. The\nHeaviside step makes the change tangible: if  is zero to the left of\nthe origin and one to the right, then\n\nIt has no classical derivative at the origin, but its\ndistributional derivative\nis the Dirac mass.\n\nSobolev weak derivatives impose an\nadditional requirement: the distributional derivative must be represented by\nan  function. Thus  controls the integrability of both\nthe function and its first weak derivatives. The step function illustrates\nthe distinction: its Dirac derivative is a distribution, but is not an\n function across the jump. Distributional and Sobolev derivatives solve\nrelated problems with different amounts of regularity.\n\nFor the elliptic equation , moving one\nderivative onto a test function gives\n\nThe left side is a bilinear form in  and . This lets\nLax–Milgram apply once the form is bounded\nand coercive and the right side is a continuous functional. Monotone\noperators, compactness, and Fredholm theory extend this approach to other\nequations. The zero-boundary space  encodes a homogeneous\nDirichlet condition by closure of compactly supported smooth functions. A\ntrace theorem, when available on the domain, relates that condition to the\nboundary values of the function.\n\nVariational formulations express the equation as an identity against test\nfunctions. When an energy is available, its first variation can yield this\nidentity. For example, Dirichlet's principle seeks a minimizer of\n\nFor an admissible variation , expand  and\ndifferentiate at . At a minimizer the result is\n\nThis is the weak Poisson equation. The calculation explains what a minimizer\nwould satisfy. To show that a\nminimizer exists, we need\ncoercivity to bound a minimizing sequence, weak compactness to extract a\nlimit, weak closedness to keep the limit admissible, and weak lower\nsemicontinuity to keep its energy minimal. The first variation and the\nexistence argument answer different parts of Dirichlet's principle.\n\nTime adds a further issue. For weak heat flow, the spatial energy naturally\ncontrols  in , while the equation controls  in\n. A typical choice is  and\n, with the dense continuous inclusions\n forming a\nGelfand triple. The time derivative is\na functional on spatial test functions; it need not itself lie in .\n\nAn  class in time has no preferred value at time zero. Lions–Magenes\ntheory recovers an -continuous representative and an\nenergy identity from the\ntwo time-space bounds. This is how initial data can become meaningful again\nafter an existence argument has passed through almost-everywhere classes.\n\nFluid mechanics adds named classes because their extra conditions do\nmathematical work. A\nLeray–Hopf solution satisfies the\nweak Navier–Stokes equations together with integrability, weak continuity, and\nan energy inequality; a suitable weak solution carries the local energy\ninequality needed for partial regularity; a\nclassical solution has enough\nregularity for pointwise calculus. A theorem about one class says nothing\nabout another until a regularity or comparison argument connects them.\n\nFor\nentropy solutions,\nan inequality selects among distributional solutions of a nonlinear\nconservation law. For\nviscosity solutions,\nhow smooth test functions touch a possibly nondifferentiable function from\nabove or below is what matters.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Introduction","header":"1.3. Solution concepts","id":"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--Solution-concepts"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Finite-Time-Singularity-Interfaces--Generic-finite-time-language":{"contents":"A trajectory  in a type equipped with a norm blows up at\n whenThis is an equation-independent predicate. It can be applied to scalar\nobservables, Banach-space solution trajectories, and Sobolev- or\n-valued evolutions.\n\nBecause the predicate concerns only the scalar function\n, blow-up can be transported by any operation\nwhose effect on norms is controlled. Multiplication by a scalar amplitude,\ncomposition with a linear isometry, and addition of an eventually bounded\nerror are three such operations; the amplitude case is the one that\nsimilarity profiles use.\n\nLet  be a seminormed module over a normed field . If\n as  and a profile family\n satisfieseventually for some , then  blows up in the norm of .\nThis is the quotient-friendly route for Sobolev and  profiles: once a\ndilation or pullback has been defined on the function space itself, the\nargument needs only a lower norm bound and never chooses a pointwise\nrepresentative.\n\nBy the scalar-multiplication norm identity, the norm of the product is\n. Eventually this is bounded below by\n. Multiplication by the positive constant  preserves\nconvergence to , so the product norm also diverges.\n\nThe amplitude theorem runs in one direction only: a divergent amplitude\nproduces blow-up. A linear isometry preserves the norm exactly, so with it\nthe transfer is an equivalence.\n\nA linear isometric embedding preserves and reflects norm blow-up. This lets a\ntrajectory move between isometric models of a Sobolev or  space without\nchoosing representatives or reproving its asymptotic norm behavior.\n\nUnfold norm blow-up and composition. A linear isometry satisfies\n pointwise, so the two convergence statements\nare identical.\n\nAn isometry changes the trajectory without changing its norm. A singular\nleading term plus a controlled correction changes the norm by an eventually\nbounded amount.\n\nIf  is eventually bounded as , then\n blows up at  if and only if  does. Thus an eventually bounded\nremainder neither creates nor removes norm blow-up.\n\nSuppose  eventually. To force\n above a threshold , first force\n above . The triangle inequality\n then gives the desired\nbound. The reverse implication is the same argument after using\n.\n\nA trajectory whose norm blows up at  has no left-continuous extension\nthrough . More precisely, there is no function continuous within\n at  that agrees with the trajectory for every .\n\nA continuous extension  would converge to the finite value  from\nwithin . Restrict this convergence to times below  and use\nthe pointwise agreement  there. This gives a finite left limit for\n, contradicting norm blow-up.\n\nThat obstruction uses the norm alone. Paired with an existence statement for\nintegral curves it becomes a continuation alternative: for an autonomous \nfield on a complete space, an integral curve that fails to continue past a\nfinite time can have no limit there, and norm blow-up is one way for the\nlimit to fail.\n\nLet  be a complete real normed space, , , and let\n be an integral curve of the autonomous equationIf  as  and  is  at , then there\nare  and  with  on  and\n for all . The same conclusion holds when\n is Cauchy as  and  is  at every point, and,\nwithout completeness, when a local solution through  on some\n is supplied and  is merely continuous\nat .Conversely, in any normed group, if  as\n, then no  with  for all\n is continuous within  at . The same holds along\nany nontrivial clock  for which\n, and more basically a trajectory whose\nnorm tends to infinity along a nontrivial filter converges to no point.The obstruction half is the no-extension theorem for norm blow-up stated\nwithout the blow-up predicate, together with its reparametrized and\nraw-filter variants.\n\nFor continuation, extend  to  by the value  at . On\n the derivative  tends to , so  has left\nderivative  at . Since  is  at , the\nPicard–Lindelöf theorem supplies a local solution  with  on\n. Define  for  and\n for . Away from  the curve  locally coincides with a\nsolution; at  its left and right derivatives are both , so \nis differentiable there with the correct derivative. In the Cauchy form,\ncompleteness produces the limit .For the obstruction, a left-continuous extension  would give\n as , hence\n, contradicting divergence of\nthe norm.\n\nEverything so far concerns a single trajectory in a normed space. A spatial\nfield  has no preferred norm; the pointwise supremum over  is\nthe least committal choice, and since a slice may be unbounded the supremum\nhas to be allowed the value .\n\nFor a field , the pointwise spatial supremum is the\nextended nonnegative realIn Lean this is ⨆ x, ENNReal.ofReal ‖u x t‖. An unbounded spatial slice\ntherefore has value . The limit  alone does\nnot identify a blow-up time: the stationary field  on\n has  at every time.SpatialSupNormBlowsUpAt u T requires both this limit as \nand finite spatial slices for all  sufficiently close to .\nThe finite bound may depend on ; no uniform bound in time is imposed.\nGeneral divergence statements use Tendsto (spatialSupNorm u) directly,\nso they also apply to fields with unbounded slices.This is a pointwise supremum: changing one value of  can change .\nSobolev and  trajectories use norm divergence at the finite horizon in their own normed\nstate space, for example through the scalar-amplitude blow-up criterion.\n\nA supremum dominates each of its terms, so divergence of the field along one\nspatial sample, which may move with time, already forces divergence of\n.\n\nIf the norm along a possibly time-dependent spatial sample  tends to\n, then the extended pointwise spatial supremum also tends to\n. This limit transfer holds along any filter of times.\nFor the left limit at , the single-point blow-up criterion also takes\neventual finiteness of the slices and concludes finite-time blow-up.\n\nFor every time , the sampled quantity\n is one term in the supremum\ndefining . The sampled norm tends to , hence so does its\nENNReal.ofReal image. Monotonicity of convergence to the top element then\ntransfers the limit to . Together with the supplied finite-slice\ncondition, this proves divergence of the spatial supremum norm.\n\nFor a similarity ansatz the natural sample is the physical point\n, whose similarity coordinate stays at ;\nalong it the profile value is frozen and only the amplitude\n varies. Every ansatz divergence theorem in the\nequation-specific chapters works this way. A positive amplitude exponent\nand one nonzero profile value suffice for divergence. A bounded profile\nalso gives finite spatial slices, so the field satisfies the blow-up\npredicate.\n\nA pointwise profile , amplitude exponent , spatial\nexponent , center , and time  determineThe pointwise construction is polymorphic in the spatial and value types:\nscalar, vector-valued, complex-valued, and system profiles can share it when\nthe required real scaling operations exist. This is a pointwise profile\nconstruction; it assumes no differentiability until an equation-specific\ntheorem requires it.An  or Sobolev profile is instead an almost-everywhere equivalence\nclass. Its similarity theory needs dilation pullbacks on that quotient and a\nweak form of the reduced equation, instead of pointwise composition.\nOnce the quotient-level pullback is available, the scalar-amplitude blow-up criterion\nsupplies the abstract amplitude-times-profile step.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Finite-Time Behavior\u0009Finite-Time Growth, Lifespan, and Similarity\u0009Finite-Time Singularity Interfaces","header":"6.7.1.5.2. Generic finite-time language","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Finite-Time-Singularity-Interfaces--Generic-finite-time-language"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Sobolev___-Morrey___-Poincar______-and-Hardy-Inequalities--Sobolev-Embeddings":{"contents":"Sobolev embedding asks how much integrability follows from control of weak\nderivatives. Below the dimension threshold, the first-order answer is governed\nby the Sobolev conjugate . At order , the same gain produces\n while . At the critical relation , the\nfinite-target theorems assume  and . On bounded\noperational domains they give membership in every  with\n; a general  conclusion fails, as a\ntwo-dimensional  example shows. The one-dimensional \nendpoint is stronger and belongs to the Morrey theory.\n\nThe exponent  is the one forced by scaling, and the\nGagliardo–Nirenberg–Sobolev inequality shows that it is attained (Evans,\n2010, §5.6.1, Theorem 1; ,\nTheorem 4.12).\n\nFor a useful numerical instance,  and  give .\nThus an  function on the whole space, or on a bounded operational\n domain, belongs to . Its cubic nonlinearity is then square\nintegrable:\n\n\n\nThis is a common reason to use an embedding: the equation contains ,\nwhile the energy estimate initially controls only  and its first\nderivatives in .\n\nOn , a closed-graph argument makes the subcritical inclusion a\ncontinuous linear map. On domains, either a supplied extension operator\ntransfers the global theorem, or a concrete  atlas is used one\nfirst-order slice at a time; the atlas iteration produces continuous linear\nmaps between completed graph spaces whose rows agree almost everywhere with\nthe original derivatives.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings\u0009Sobolev, Morrey, Poincaré, and Hardy Inequalities","header":"4.3.4.4. Sobolev Embeddings","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Sobolev___-Morrey___-Poincar______-and-Hardy-Inequalities--Sobolev-Embeddings"}});